<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consult-laptop.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consult-laptop-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consult-laptop.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal FAQ</span></nav><p class="kicker">Answers</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal FAQ for New Hampshire</h1><p class="lede">25 plain answers, grouped by topic — the process, money, timing, working with us and New Hampshire specifics.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><nav class="jump" aria-label="FAQ topics"><a href="#faq-1">Starting out</a><a href="#faq-2">Money and fees</a><a href="#faq-3">Clocks and deadlines</a><a href="#faq-4">Hiring us</a><a href="#faq-5">Granite State specifics</a></nav><h2 id="faq-1">Starting out</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal, in plain terms?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It’s a process written into most property policies for settling a disagreement about the dollar amount of a covered loss. Each side names an appraiser, the two pick an umpire, and any two of the three can sign a binding figure.</p></div></details><details><summary>When does appraisal make sense?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>When the insurer agrees the damage is covered but you disagree on what it costs to fix, and the gap is meaningful. If the argument is about whether something is covered at all, appraisal usually isn’t the right tool.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I demand appraisal if my whole claim was denied?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually not on its own, because a full denial is a coverage dispute rather than a disagreement about amount. Talk to a New Hampshire attorney about a denial; appraisal may come later if coverage is accepted.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does the insurer have to agree to appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Under most appraisal clauses either side can demand it in writing, and the other side is expected to take part. Insurers sometimes object on coverage or procedural grounds, which is a legal question for an attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I need my own estimate before demanding appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It helps a great deal. A contractor’s estimate or an independent estimate review shows where the gap is and whether it is large enough to justify the cost of appraisal.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-2">Money and fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How are appraisers paid?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Each side pays its own appraiser. Arrangements vary — hourly, flat fee or a percentage of the award — so get the terms in writing before you sign.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most appraisal clauses split the umpire’s fees and expenses equally between you and the insurer. Check your policy for its exact wording.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it on a small gap?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often not. When the difference is a few thousand dollars, appraiser and umpire fees can absorb much of it, so run the numbers first.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can the award come in lower than the insurer’s estimate?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It can. The panel values the loss independently and isn’t bound by either side’s number, which is one reason to start with an honest, well-supported estimate.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does the award account for my deductible and depreciation?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The award normally sets the amount of loss, often broken into replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value. The insurer then applies the deductible, earlier payments and policy limits when it pays.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-3">Clocks and deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does an appraisal take in New Hampshire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Straightforward residential appraisals often wrap up within a few months. Snow cover, scheduling, large commercial losses and umpire involvement can stretch that out.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to demand appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Some policies set one and many don’t, but courts can look at whether a demand came within a reasonable time. Don’t sit on a dispute; check your policy and ask an attorney if the timing is tight.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal pause the suit-limitation period?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not necessarily. Many policies set a contractual window to sue and appraisal may not stop it, so check your policy and talk to a New Hampshire attorney before the date gets close.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can winter hold up the inspection?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Deep snow can hide roof and siding damage, so appraisers sometimes inspect what they can and return after the thaw to measure the rest.</p></div></details><details><summary>How soon does the insurer pay after an award?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Payment timing depends on the policy and the insurer’s process, and many forms state a period after the award is filed. Recoverable depreciation is often paid later, once repairs are documented.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-4">Hiring us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who do you represent?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>When we’re appointed as your appraiser, you retain us and we prepare the valuation on your claim. On separate claims we may serve as a neutral umpire, where we represent neither side.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are you a public adjuster or a law firm?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Neither. New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser handles appraisal-clause work, estimate reviews and umpire assignments; we don’t give legal advice or adjust claims outside appraisal.</p></div></details><details><summary>What should I send for a first review?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Your declarations page, the full policy if you have it, the insurer’s complete estimate, any letters from the insurer, photos and your contractor’s estimate.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you work across the whole state?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. We take claims in all ten counties, from the Seacoast and the Merrimack Valley to the Lakes Region and the Great North Woods.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you be the umpire on a claim where you’re my appraiser?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. An umpire has to be neutral, so a firm acting as your appraiser can’t also be the umpire on that claim.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-5">Granite State specifics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurance companies in New Hampshire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The New Hampshire Insurance Department in Concord, led by the Insurance Commissioner. It accepts consumer complaints and inquiries about insurers doing business in the state.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does New Hampshire have a bad-faith penalty statute?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>New Hampshire doesn’t have a broad first-party bad-faith penalty statute; remedies depend on the policy, contract law and the facts. Ask a New Hampshire attorney about your situation.</p></div></details><details><summary>What does RSA chapter 417 cover?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It addresses unfair insurance trade practices, including unfair claim settlement practices such as misrepresenting policy provisions. Enforcement is largely administrative through the Insurance Department; whether you have a private claim is a question for an attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who appoints the umpire if the appraisers can’t agree?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies say a court of record where the property is located may appoint one. In New Hampshire that is generally the Superior Court in the county where the property sits.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does a New Hampshire FAIR Plan policy include appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many property forms do, but check the FAIR Plan policy you hold for its own appraisal wording and conditions.</p></div></details></div></article>
<aside class="side"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-t">A free first look</h2><p>A free first conversation. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="big-tel" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-gold btn-block" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-t">Learn</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/how-nh-appraisal-works/">How NH appraisal works<svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-faq/" aria-current="page">Appraisal FAQ<svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/nh-claim-glossary/">Claim glossary<svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-guides/">Guides<svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></li></ul></div></aside></div><section class="band"><div class="wrap band-in rv"><div><h2>Still wondering about something?</h2><p>Call or email — we’re glad to talk it through.</p></div><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
